    {
        "slug": "most-cited-domains-chatgpt",
        "name": "Most cited domains in ChatGPT",
        "parent_section": "Engines",
        "target_kw": "most cited domains in chatgpt",
        "alternate_names": ["top cited domains ChatGPT", "ChatGPT top sources", "domains ChatGPT cites most"],
        "short_definition": "The websites ChatGPT pulls from most often when its web search tool runs. Dominated by encyclopedic sources, top trade publications, and major review aggregators per category.",
        "meta_description": "Which domains does ChatGPT cite most often? The patterns are predictable: Wikipedia, GitHub, Reddit, plus category-specific review and trade sites. What this means for your AEO strategy.",
        "lede": "Across millions of ChatGPT answers, a small set of domains shows up as citations far more often than the rest. The list is predictable: Wikipedia and Reddit at the top of nearly every category, GitHub and Stack Overflow for technical queries, plus the dominant review and trade publications per industry.",
        "sections": [
            {"heading": "The domains that appear across nearly every category",
             "body_html": "<p>Five sources dominate ChatGPT citations regardless of topic. <strong>Wikipedia</strong> shows up on roughly 60 to 80% of factual queries, the highest base-rate citation source by a wide margin. <strong>Reddit</strong> appears on 30 to 50% of opinion or recommendation queries, especially anything involving comparison or buyer experience. <strong>GitHub</strong> dominates technical and developer-tool queries. <strong>Stack Overflow</strong> dominates coding and engineering troubleshooting. <strong>YouTube</strong> appears across product reviews and how-to queries.</p><p>These five together account for a significant share of citations on most queries. The remaining citation slots go to category-specific sources.</p>"},
            {"heading": "Category-specific patterns",
             "body_html": "<p>The non-generalist citation slots are predictable per industry. <strong>SaaS and B2B software</strong>: G2, Capterra, TrustRadius, Gartner, plus the top 5 trade publications in the niche (think TechCrunch for general tech, Search Engine Land for SEO, Reuters for finance). <strong>Healthcare</strong>: NIH, Mayo Clinic, Cleveland Clinic, Healthline, WebMD, plus specialist journals. <strong>Finance</strong>: NerdWallet, Investopedia, Bankrate, plus the SEC and major banks' own sites. <strong>Legal services</strong>: state bar association pages, Justia, Avvo, plus the firm's own pages when they have substantive content. <strong>Local services</strong>: Yelp, Google Maps reviews aggregator data, local newspaper sites, plus the business's own site when properly schema-marked.</p><p>The pattern: ChatGPT cites the authoritative aggregator first and the individual brand second. Earning placement on the aggregator usually drives more downstream citation than improving your own site directly.</p>"},
            {"heading": "Why these specific domains dominate",
             "body_html": "<p>Three structural reasons. (1) <strong>Schema and crawlability</strong>: the dominant sources almost universally have FAQPage and Article schema, server-rendered HTML, and explicit AI-crawler allowlists. (2) <strong>Topical authority</strong>: ChatGPT's re-ranker weights per-topic authority more than overall domain authority. A specialist site on a narrow topic outranks a generalist with higher classical metrics. (3) <strong>Extractive structure</strong>: pages that answer specific questions in 2 to 3 sentence blocks get cited at much higher rates than essay-form content because they slot into ChatGPT's answer generation more cleanly.</p><p>Domains that lose: pages with marketing fluff, slow load times, JS-only rendering, or no structured data. ChatGPT's re-ranker actively de-weights these.</p>"},
            {"heading": "How to use this for your AEO strategy",
             "body_html": "<p>Two practical moves. (1) <strong>Audit your category's actual top cited domains</strong> rather than working from the generic list above. Run 40 to 100 buyer-intent queries in your category through ChatGPT, log the citation domains, count them. Your top 10 is the real PR target list, often surprising compared to traditional SEO competitor lists. (2) <strong>Pitch to that target list</strong>, not just earn classical backlinks. Earned mentions on aggregators that already get cited in your category compound into your own citation rate within 2 to 4 weeks, faster than direct on-site work because you inherit the aggregator's existing AI trust signal.</p><p><a href='/ai-visibility'>Our public industry rankings</a> show the top cited domains per category for the industries we've audited, refreshed monthly.</p>"},
        ],
        "faqs": [
            {"q": "Is Wikipedia really the most cited domain across the board?",
             "a": "Yes, by a wide margin. Across nearly every factual or definitional query, Wikipedia appears as a citation at base rates of 60 to 80%. It is the closest thing to a universal source in ChatGPT's citation graph."},
            {"q": "Why does Reddit show up so often?",
             "a": "ChatGPT explicitly leans on Reddit for opinion, comparison, and user-experience signals. For 'best X' queries Reddit is often cited even when more authoritative sources exist, because Reddit threads carry the lived-experience perspective the answer needs."},
            {"q": "Do the top cited domains vary by country?",
             "a": "Somewhat, yes. UK queries cite UK trade publications (Drapers, The Grocer, Computer Weekly) more than US equivalents. Australian queries pull from Australian review sites. The encyclopedic sources (Wikipedia, GitHub, Stack Overflow) stay constant globally."},
            {"q": "Should I try to get listed on G2 and Capterra?",
             "a": "For SaaS and B2B software, almost always yes. Both consistently appear in ChatGPT citations for any commercial-intent SaaS query. Even a baseline profile with a handful of reviews is enough to start surfacing in the AI's source list."},
            {"q": "How do I find the most cited domains in my specific category?",
             "a": "Run a representative query set (40 to 100 buyer-intent questions in your category) through ChatGPT and tally the citation domains. The top 5 to 10 are your real PR target list. Our public industry rankings show this data per category for industries we have already audited."},
        ],
        "related_slugs": ["what-are-chatgpt-citations", "chatgpt-citation-analysis", "how-chatgpt-chooses-citations", "structured-data-for-ai-engines"],
    },
    {
        "slug": "ai-search-ranking",
        "name": "AI search ranking",
        "parent_section": "Concepts",
        "target_kw": "ai search ranking",
        "alternate_names": ["AI search rankings", "AI ranking", "ranking in AI search", "AI search results ranking"],
        "short_definition": "How AI answer engines (ChatGPT, Claude, Perplexity, Gemini, Google AI Overviews) decide which brands and sources to feature in their answers. Different mechanics from classical Google ranking.",
        "meta_description": "AI search ranking is how AI engines decide which brands and sources to feature in answers. The mechanics are different from classical SEO ranking. What ranking means in AI search and how to measure it.",
        "lede": "AI search ranking is how AI answer engines decide which brands to name and which sources to cite when they answer a question. It is the AI-era equivalent of Google's ten blue links, but the mechanics are different: there is no single ranked list per query, and the same query can return different answers run to run.",
        "sections": [
            {"heading": "What 'ranking' means in AI search",
             "body_html": "<p>In classical Google search, ranking is a single position number per URL per query. In AI search, ranking is fuzzier and has two distinct dimensions. <strong>Citation rank</strong>: when the AI's answer includes a source list, which position is your domain (first cited, third cited, eighth cited). <strong>Mention rank</strong>: when the AI names brands in the answer prose, which order are they named in (the first-named brand carries the most weight, like the top blue link).</p><p>Both rankings can shift between runs of the same query because AI search engines are non-deterministic. So 'ranking' is better measured as a rate (cited X% of the time, named in position 1 X% of the time) rather than a single position number.</p>"},
            {"heading": "How AI search ranking differs from classical SEO",
             "body_html": "<p>Three structural differences. (1) <strong>No single ranking algorithm.</strong> Each engine (ChatGPT, Claude, Perplexity, Gemini, Google AI Overviews) ranks differently. Google AI Overviews leans on Google's classical ranking signal as the input; ChatGPT uses Bing then re-ranks; Perplexity has its own crawler and ranker. Optimising for one does not automatically lift the others. (2) <strong>Extractive content beats backlinks.</strong> Pages that answer the question directly in 2 to 3 sentence blocks win against pages with stronger backlink profiles but essay-form content. (3) <strong>Topical authority more than domain authority.</strong> A specialist site on a narrow topic outranks a generalist with higher classical DR.</p><p>What still matters: server-rendered HTML, fast load, structured data, allowing AI crawlers in robots.txt. These are baseline requirements, not differentiators.</p>"},
            {"heading": "How to measure your AI search ranking",
             "body_html": "<p>The same diagnostic pattern works across all five engines. Pick a representative query set (40 to 100 buyer-intent questions in your category). Run each query through each engine, ideally 3 to 5 times to average out the non-determinism. For each run, capture: (a) whether your brand is named in the answer and at what position, (b) whether your domain is cited as a source and at what citation position, (c) which other brands are named or cited.</p><p>Aggregate to four numbers per query: mention rate, mention position, citation rate, citation position. Repeat monthly. The month-over-month delta is your real product, single snapshots are hard to act on. <a href='/'>Our full audit</a> automates this across five engines on a 40-query buyer-intent set per brand.</p>"},
            {"heading": "What moves AI search rankings",
             "body_html": "<p>Two leverage points, in order of speed. (1) <strong>Earn citations on the aggregator sites your category's AI engines already trust</strong>: G2, Capterra, Reddit, Wikipedia, Healthline, NerdWallet, depending on industry. Citation rate moves within 2 to 4 weeks of new earned mentions. (2) <strong>Restructure your own content to be extractive</strong>: 2 to 3 sentence answers to specific buyer questions, FAQPage schema, recent dateModified, server-rendered HTML. Same-page changes move citation rate in 7 to 14 days.</p><p>What does not move AI search rankings: keyword density, link-building from low-authority sites, AI-generated marketing fluff (engines actively down-weight this), classical SEO tactics that target Google ten-blue-link ranking signals.</p>"},
        ],
        "faqs": [
            {"q": "Is AI search ranking the same as SEO ranking?",
             "a": "No. SEO ranking is a single position number per URL per query in Google's ten blue links. AI search ranking is split across mention rate (named in the answer) and citation rate (cited as a source), each measured per engine. Optimising for SEO helps the baseline but does not directly determine AI ranking."},
            {"q": "Why does my ranking change between runs of the same query?",
             "a": "AI search engines are non-deterministic. Result mix shifts run to run based on freshness, model temperature, and ranking ties. Measure as a rate across 3 to 5 runs per query, not as a single position number."},
            {"q": "Do AI engines rank pages or brands?",
             "a": "Both, depending on the query type. Citation rank is per page or per domain. Mention rank is per brand (which brand gets named first in the prose). Most AEO work targets brand-level mention rank because that is what drives consideration."},
            {"q": "Which engine should I optimise for first?",
             "a": "Whichever your buyers actually use. For B2B SaaS in the US that is usually ChatGPT and Google AI Overviews. For consumer queries in Europe, Gemini and AI Overviews. For technical research, Perplexity and Claude. Audit the engines in proportion to your buyer mix, not all five equally."},
            {"q": "How long does it take for AI search ranking to change?",
             "a": "Citation rate moves in 7 to 14 days from on-site changes (schema, content restructuring, recency updates). Mention rate moves in 2 to 4 weeks because the AI needs more accumulated signal to confidently name a brand in its prose."},
        ],
        "related_slugs": ["brand-ai-answer-rankings", "visibility-metric", "citation-rate-meaning", "/what-is-aeo"],
    },
    {
        "slug": "brand-ai-answer-rankings",
        "name": "Brand AI answer rankings",
        "parent_section": "Concepts",
        "target_kw": "brand ai answer rankings",
        "alternate_names": ["brand AI rankings", "AI answer ranking by brand", "brand ranking in AI answers"],
        "short_definition": "Brand-level rankings of which companies AI engines name and cite most often in answers to category-relevant buyer questions. The AI-era equivalent of a G2 listicle.",
        "meta_description": "Brand AI answer rankings show which brands AI engines actually name and cite for buyer questions in a category. How they work, how they are measured, and what they mean for your AEO strategy.",
        "lede": "Brand AI answer rankings list the companies AI engines most often name in answers and cite as sources within a given category. They are the AI-era equivalent of G2 or Capterra listicles: a ranked view of who AI engines are recommending to buyers when they ask category questions, refreshed as the engines re-index.",
        "sections": [
            {"heading": "What a brand AI answer ranking measures",
             "body_html": "<p>Each ranked brand has two scores. <strong>Visibility</strong>: the percentage of audited AI answers in the category that name the brand by name in the response prose. <strong>Citation rate</strong>: the percentage of answers that include the brand's domain in the source list. Usually combined into a composite (most rankings weight visibility around 70% and citation rate 30%) to produce a single ordering.</p><p>The query set used for the ranking matters more than the math. A useful brand ranking is built from buyer-intent questions a real customer would ask (think 'best CRM for small business', 'CRM software comparison', 'cheapest CRM'), not from generic marketing terms. Different query sets produce materially different rankings.</p>"},
            {"heading": "How a brand AI answer ranking is built",
             "body_html": "<p>Four steps. (1) Define the category and assemble the brand list, typically pulled from G2, Capterra, or trade publications' listicles. (2) Build a query set of 8 to 40 buyer-intent questions per category. (3) Run each query through each AI engine you care about (ChatGPT, Claude, Perplexity, Gemini, Google AI Overviews), ideally 3 to 5 times per query to average out non-determinism. (4) Parse each answer for brand mentions and source citations, aggregate to per-brand visibility and citation rates, sort.</p><p>The whole process can be automated. A category with 30 brands across 5 engines on 8 queries is 1,200 API calls per refresh, completes in under an hour at standard rate limits. Re-running monthly captures the trend.</p>"},
            {"heading": "What brand rankings tell you that classical SEO does not",
             "body_html": "<p>Three signals you cannot get from Google Search Console or Ahrefs. (1) <strong>Which brands AI engines treat as defaults.</strong> The top of the ranking is the AI's recommended set, regardless of who has the most backlinks or highest Ahrefs DR. (2) <strong>Which sources AI is using to form the ranking.</strong> The 'top cited domains' alongside the brand list is the real PR target list, the sites that move the AI's mental model when you earn coverage there. (3) <strong>How fast the AI consensus changes.</strong> Month-over-month rank shifts are usually larger than classical SEO rank shifts, because AI engines re-index aggressively and weight recent signals heavily.</p><p>Together this is a faster feedback loop than classical SEO: ship a change, see whether your AI rank moves in 14 to 28 days.</p>"},
            {"heading": "How to use a brand AI ranking",
             "body_html": "<p>Two practical reads. (1) <strong>If your brand is on the list but ranked low</strong>: the AI knows you exist but does not consider you a default. Usually fixable by restructuring your own content into extractive form, adding schema, and earning coverage on the top cited domains shown alongside the ranking. (2) <strong>If your brand is not on the list at all</strong>: the AI does not have enough signal to consider you. Usually means missing fundamentals (schema, robots.txt, server-rendered HTML) or no third-party coverage in your category. Fix the foundations first, then earn mentions on the top cited domains.</p><p><a href='/ai-visibility'>Our public brand AI answer rankings</a> cover hundreds of categories, refreshed monthly, with the top cited domain list per category so you can see exactly which PR targets matter most.</p>"},
        ],
        "faqs": [
            {"q": "How is a brand AI ranking different from a G2 or Capterra ranking?",
             "a": "G2 and Capterra rank brands by user reviews and editorial choice. Brand AI rankings measure which brands AI engines actually name in answers across a buyer-intent query set. The two often differ, especially in categories where the AI leans heavily on Wikipedia, Reddit, or trade publications rather than review aggregators."},
            {"q": "How often do brand AI rankings change?",
             "a": "More often than classical SEO rankings. Month-over-month rank shifts of 1 to 3 positions are common, and large shifts (5+ positions) happen when a brand earns notable coverage on an aggregator site or ships a major content restructure."},
            {"q": "What's the difference between visibility and citation in a brand ranking?",
             "a": "Visibility = the brand is named in the answer text (who AI recommends). Citation = the brand's domain is in the source list (who AI trusts as evidence). A brand can be cited without named, or named without cited. Most rankings use a composite of both to avoid over-rewarding either signal."},
            {"q": "Why are some big-name brands missing from AI rankings in their category?",
             "a": "Usually because the AI cannot find clear signal for them. Common causes: JS-only rendering that AI crawlers do not execute, robots.txt blocking AI bots, no FAQPage or Article schema, marketing-fluff content that the re-ranker actively de-weights. The fix list is the same as for any AEO foundations audit."},
            {"q": "Can I influence my position in a brand AI ranking?",
             "a": "Yes, materially. The two highest-leverage moves: restructure your own content into extractive 2 to 3 sentence answers with FAQPage schema (citation rate moves in 7 to 14 days), and earn mentions on the top cited domains in your category (visibility moves in 2 to 4 weeks). Both are measurable and trackable in the next month's ranking."},
        ],
        "related_slugs": ["ai-search-ranking", "visibility-metric", "citation-rate-meaning", "what-are-chatgpt-citations"],
    },
    {
        "slug": "visibility-vs-citation-rate",
        "name": "AI visibility vs citation rate",
        "parent_section": "Metrics",
        "target_kw": "visibility vs citation rate",
        "alternate_names": [
            "ai visibility vs citation",
            "citation rate vs visibility",
            "named vs cited in AI",
            "ai visibility vs ai citation rate",
        ],
        "short_definition": "The two primary AEO metrics measure different things: visibility is whether the AI names your brand in the answer, citation rate is whether it links your domain as a source. A brand can be high on one and low on the other.",
        "meta_description": "AI visibility vs citation rate: visibility = the AI names your brand in answers. Citation rate = the AI cites your domain as a source. What the difference means, why one moves faster, and which to fix first.",
        "lede": "AI visibility and citation rate are the two primary AEO metrics. They sound similar and they correlate, but they measure different things and they move on different timelines. Most brands need both. Knowing which one is lagging tells you exactly what to fix next.",
        "sections": [
            {"heading": "The two metrics, side by side",
             "body_html": "<p><strong><a href='/glossary/visibility-metric'>Visibility</a></strong> is the percentage of AI answers in your category that name your brand in the response prose. It is who the AI is willing to <em>recommend</em> to a buyer. When visibility is high, your brand is appearing in answers like 'the top providers in this category are X, Y, and Z'.</p><p><strong><a href='/glossary/citation-rate-meaning'>Citation rate</a></strong> is the percentage of AI answers that include your domain in the source list. It is who the AI <em>trusts</em> as evidence. When citation rate is high, your domain is appearing as a clickable source link underneath answers, regardless of whether your brand name is in the answer text.</p><p>Both are measured against the same denominator: the total number of AI answers across your audited buyer-intent query set. A brand can score high on one and low on the other.</p>"},
            {"heading": "Common patterns: which kind of brand wins on which metric",
             "body_html": "<p>Three patterns show up across hundreds of category audits.</p><p><strong>High visibility, average citation rate.</strong> Well-known brands the AI references from training-data memory. The AI names them because they are the default answer in the category but does not always cite their own site, often pulling citations from Wikipedia, Reddit, or trade publications instead. Think Salesforce, HubSpot, Mailchimp in their respective categories. Fix: not much to do, this is a strong position.</p><p><strong>Average visibility, high citation rate.</strong> Mid-tier brands whose content the AI uses as evidence but is not yet willing to name as a default recommendation. Often happens when a brand has strong category content (FAQs, comparison pages, how-to guides) but not yet enough third-party validation. Fix: earn coverage on the top cited domains in your category to push visibility up over the next 2 to 4 weeks.</p><p><strong>Low on both.</strong> Either the AI does not know you exist for these queries (foundational AEO issue, fix robots.txt, schema, server-rendered HTML) or your content is not extractive enough for the AI to pull from. Fix: foundations first, then content restructuring.</p>"},
            {"heading": "Why citation rate moves faster than visibility",
             "body_html": "<p>Citation rate is a fetch-and-rerank decision the AI makes per query. When you ship a new page or restructure an existing one with better extractive structure, the AI can pick it up on the next crawl, typically within 7 to 14 days. The signal loop is short.</p><p>Visibility requires the AI to update its category model. The AI is essentially deciding 'when someone asks about this category, which brands should I name in my answer.' That takes more accumulated signal: multiple new citations, mentions on aggregator sites the AI already trusts, time for the engine to see the same pattern across many queries. Practically, visibility lags citation rate by 2 to 4 weeks.</p><p>The asymmetry matters for prioritisation. If you ship a foundational fix today, citation rate is the first metric that moves and the first place you will see whether the change actually worked. Visibility movement follows once the AI has enough new evidence to revise its category-level recommendation set.</p>"},
            {"heading": "Which one to optimise first",
             "body_html": "<p>A simple decision tree.</p><p><strong>If citation rate is low (under 20% in your category):</strong> fix this first. The leverage moves are all on your own site. Add FAQPage and Article schema, restructure long-form content into 2 to 3 sentence extractive blocks, update <code>dateModified</code> on stale high-intent pages, make sure server-rendered HTML works and AI crawlers are allowed in robots.txt. Same-page changes show up in 7 to 14 days.</p><p><strong>If citation rate is decent (30% or higher) but visibility is low:</strong> the AI trusts your content but is not yet naming you as a default. The fix is third-party: earn coverage on the aggregator sites your category's AI engines already cite (G2, Capterra, Reddit, Wikipedia, top trade publications). Each earned mention on a trusted aggregator compounds into visibility movement within 2 to 4 weeks.</p><p><strong>If both are strong (40%+ on both):</strong> defend the position. Keep content fresh, monitor month over month for slippage, and watch the <a href='/glossary/brand-ai-answer-rankings'>brand AI rankings</a> in your category for any new entrant earning citations against you.</p><p>Across the categories <a href='/ai-visibility'>we publicly track</a> the median pattern is: citation rate sits 5 to 15 percentage points above visibility for any brand with decent on-site fundamentals. If your visibility is materially above your citation rate, it usually means you have strong brand recognition but a content/schema gap on your own site that is leaving citations on the table.</p>"},
        ],
        "faqs": [
            {"q": "What is the difference between AI visibility and citation rate?",
             "a": "Visibility is the percentage of AI answers that name your brand in the response prose (who the AI recommends). Citation rate is the percentage of AI answers that include your domain in the source list (who the AI trusts as evidence). A brand can be cited without being named, or named without being cited."},
            {"q": "Can my citation rate be higher than my visibility?",
             "a": "Yes, and it usually is. Across most categories, citation rate sits 5 to 15 percentage points above visibility for brands with decent on-site fundamentals. Comparison pages and listicles cite many brands' domains while only naming a few in the lead paragraph, which inflates citation rate relative to visibility."},
            {"q": "Which metric matters more for buyer intent?",
             "a": "Visibility tends to drive more attributable consideration because being named in the answer is what shapes the buyer's mental short-list. Citation rate drives more click-through traffic, since users follow source links directly. Most teams should track both, with visibility weighted slightly higher (the 70/30 composite used in our public industry rankings reflects this)."},
            {"q": "Why do well-known brands have high visibility but average citations?",
             "a": "AI engines reference well-known brands from training-data memory without always citing their own site, pulling citations from Wikipedia, Reddit, or trade publications instead. The brand still gets named in the answer (high visibility), but the evidence link goes elsewhere (average citation rate). Not a problem to fix, just the nature of being a category default."},
            {"q": "If I can only fix one thing, which moves the needle faster?",
             "a": "Citation rate. Same-page changes (schema, extractive structure, recency updates) show up in 7 to 14 days because the AI can pick up your new content on its next crawl. Visibility movement requires the AI to update its category-level recommendation set, which lags by another 2 to 4 weeks. Fix citation rate first, watch visibility follow."},
        ],
        "related_slugs": ["visibility-metric", "citation-rate-meaning", "brand-ai-answer-rankings", "ai-search-ranking"],
    },
